I have a problem with removing an item from an array in javascript.

Here is my code:

var arr = ['one', 'two', 'three'];

arr.remove('two');

console.log(arr);

The result is: ['one', 'two', 'three']

But I want it to be ['one', 'three']. How can I do this?

Remove the item with the index of the item you want to remove:

arr.splice(arr.indexOf('two'), 1);

Note that the splice method mutates the array, so you don't need to reassign it to the variable arr.

• That's not the best solution. It will fail if there are multiple 'two' values. – Juan Mendes Apr 29 '14 at 22:28
• @JuanMendes If there are multiple values, then the result would be ['one', 'two', 'two', 'three'], which is correct. – Mike McCaughan Apr 29 '14 at 22:30
• I know, but the OP wants to remove all instances of 'two'. If that's the case, then you can just use the filter method. – Juan Mendes Apr 29 '14 at 22:31
• @JuanMendes, I guess I'm not understanding what you mean. splice will remove only the first instance of 'two', as expected. – Mike McCaughan Apr 29 '14 at 22:34
• @MikeMcCaughan Sorry, I misread it. My bad. – Juan Mendes Apr 29 '14 at 22:35

You can use the filter method:

var arr = ['one', 'two', 'three'];
var result = arr.filter(function (item) {
return item!== 'two';
});
console.log(result);

• This is a better solution if the OP really wants to remove all instances of 'two', which I think he does. – Juan Mendes Apr 29 '14 at 22:32
• @JuanMendes It would be a better solution if the OP wanted to remove all instances of 'two', but he doesn't say that. He says "remove" (singular) – Mike McCaughan Apr 29 '14 at 22:33
• @MikeMcCaughan It's implied. He wants to remove 'two' from the array, but the answer does not remove 'two' from the array. – Juan Mend

