A common subsequence is a sequence that appears in both input strings but not necessarily continuously. For example, the longest common subsequence of "ABCD" and "ACE" is "AC", which has a length of 2.

The dynamic programming approach to solving this problem involves creating a table where each cell represents the longest common subsequence ending at a particular position in the two input strings. The base case is when either of the input strings is empty, in which case the longest common subsequence is empty. The recurrence relation is as follows:

LCS(i, j) = max(LCS(i - 1, j), LCS(i, j - 1), LCS(i - 1, j - 1) + 1)
if s[i] == t[j]
LCS(i, j) = max(LCS(i - 1, j), LCS(i, j - 1))
if s[i]!= t[j]

where LCS(i, j) represents the length of the longest common subsequence between the first i characters of string s and the first j characters of string t. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(mn) where m and n are the lengths of the two input strings.

Here is an implementation of this algorithm in Python:

def lcs(s, t):
    # create a table to store the results
    table = [[0] * (len(t) + 1) for _ in range(len(s) + 1)]

    # fill in the base cases
    for i in range(len(s) + 1):
        table[i][0] = 0
    for j in range(len(t) + 1):
        table[0][j] = 0

    # fill in the remaining cells using dynamic programming
    for i in range(1, len(s) + 1):
        for j in range(1, len(t) + 1):
            if s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]:
